```
The <mark>crt*.o</mark> object files located in GCC's `sysroot` directory are included to perform these startup routines.  

Inside glibc's <mark>LIBC_START_MAIN</mark>, <mark>call_init()</mark> and <mark>call_fini()</mark> are called.  

<mark>call_init()</mark> subsequently calls <mark>__init_array_start</mark>, and <mark>call_fini()</mark> calls <mark>__fini_array_start</mark>.
```c
{
    "title": "check libc-start.c in glibc",
    "ln": true
}
---
// [!code highlight:2]
static void
call_init (int argc, char **argv, char **envp) {
...
  const size_t size = __init_array_end - __init_array_start;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < size; i++)
// [!code highlight]
      (*__init_array_start [i]) (argc, argv, envp);
...
}
...
// [!code highlight:2]
static void
call_fini (void *unused)
{
  size_t i = __fini_array_end - __fini_array_start;
  while (i-- > 0)
// [!code highlight]
    (*__fini_array_start [i]) ();
...
}
``` 
```mermaid
zenuml
    title Call init and fini sequence
    @Actor Kernel
    // 1. Kernel triggers the entry point
    Kernel -> _start: execve()

    // 2. _start prepares arguments and calls libc
    _start -> "__libc_start_main".init() {

        // 3. call_init Phase (Constructors)
        "__libc_start_main" -> "call_init".run() {
            while("for each func in .init_array") {
                "call_init" -> Contructor: execute()
            }
        }

        // 4. Actual Program Execution
        "__libc_start_main" -> main.run()

        // 5. Cleanup Phase (Destructors)
        "__libc_start_main" -> exit.process() {
            exit -> "call_fini".run() {
                while("for each func in .fini_array (Reverse)") {
                    "call_fini" -> Destructor: execute()
                }
            }
            // 6. Final Control Return to Kernel
            @return Kernel: _exit()
        }
    }
```
```adcard
```
Now, let's check which functions are actually registered and executed in <mark>init_array</mark> and <mark>fini_array</mark>.  

Looking at the ELF dynamic section of the sample program, there are `INIT_ARRAY / FINI_ARRAY` and `INIT_ARRAYSZ / FINI_ARRAYSZ`.

```bash
{
    "title": "Check ELF dynamic section"
}
---
$ readelf -d ./usage
...
// [!code highlight:4]
// [!code word:0x3da8]
// [!code word:16]
// [!code word:0x3db8]
 0x0000000000000019 (INIT_ARRAY)         0x3da8
 0x000000000000001b (INIT_ARRAYSZ)       16 (bytes)
 0x000000000000001a (FINI_ARRAY)         0x3db8
 0x000000000000001c (FINI_ARRAYSZ)       16 (bytes)
...
```
Let's use the debugger (gdb) to dump the <mark>INIT_ARRAY</mark> memory area for the size of <mark>INIT_ARRAYSZ</mark>.
```bash
{
    "title": "Inside INIT_ARRAY"
}
---
// [!code word:0x3da8]
// [!code word:0x3db8]
(gdb) x/2g 0x3da8
0x3da8:	0x0000000000001140	0x0000000000001149

(gdb) disassemble 0x0000000000001140
Dump of assembler code for function frame_dummy:
   0x0000000000001140 <+0>:	endbr64 
   0x0000000000001144 <+4>:	jmpq   0x10c0 <register_tm_clones>
End of assembler dump.

(gdb) disassemble 0x0000000000001149
// [!code word:ctor_func]
Dump of assembler code for function ctor_func:
   0x0000000000001149 <+0>:	endbr64 
   0x000000000000114d <+4>:	push   %rbp
   0x000000000000114e <+5>:	mov    %rsp,%rbp
   0x0000000000001151 <+8>:	lea    0xeac(%rip),%rdi        # 0x2004
   0x0000000000001158 <+15>:	callq  0x1050 <puts@plt>
   0x000000000000115d <+20>:	nop
   0x000000000000115e <+21>:	pop    %rbp
   0x000000000000115f <+22>:	retq   
End of assembler dump.
```

Disassembling the memory results from the `x/2g` command confirms that the start address of the explicitly declared <mark>ctor_func</mark> is registered in the <mark>INIT_ARRAY</mark>.  

This principle applies equally to <mark>FINI_ARRAY</mark>.
```bash
{
    "title": "Inside FINI_ARRAY"
}
---
// [!code word:0x3da8]
// [!code word:0x3db8]
(gdb) x/2g 0x3db8
0x3db8:	0x0000000000001100	0x0000000000001160
(gdb) disassemble 0x0000000000001100
Dump of assembler code for function __do_global_dtors_aux:
   0x0000000000001100 <+0>:	endbr64 
   0x0000000000001104 <+4>:	cmpb   $0x0,0x2f05(%rip)        # 0x4010 <completed.8059>
   0x000000000000110b <+11>:	jne    0x1138 <__do_global_dtors_aux+56>
   0x000000000000110d <+13>:	push   %rbp
   0x000000000000110e <+14>:	cmpq   $0x0,0x2ee2(%rip)        # 0x3ff8
   0x0000000000001116 <+22>:	mov    %rsp,%rbp
   0x0000000000001119 <+25>:	je     0x1127 <__do_global_dtors_aux+39>
   0x000000000000111b <+27>:	mov    0x2ee6(%rip),%rdi        # 0x4008
   0x0000000000001122 <+34>:	callq  0x1040 <__cxa_finalize@plt>
   0x0000000000001127 <+39>:	callq  0x1090 <deregister_tm_clones>
   0x000000000000112c <+44>:	movb   $0x1,0x2edd(%rip)        # 0x4010 <completed.8059>
   0x0000000000001133 <+51>:	pop    %rbp
   0x0000000000001134 <+52>:	retq   
   0x0000000000001135 <+53>:	nopl   (%rax)
   0x0000000000001138 <+56>:	retq   
   0x0000000000001139 <+57>:	nopl   0x0(%rax)
End of assembler dump.

(gdb) disassemble 0x0000000000001160
// [!code word:dtors_func]
Dump of assembler code for function dtors_func:
   0x0000000000001160 <+0>:	endbr64 
   0x0000000000001164 <+4>:	push   %rbp
   0x0000000000001165 <+5>:	mov    %rsp,%rbp
   0x0000000000001168 <+8>:	lea    0xea4(%rip),%rdi        # 0x2013
   0x000000000000116f <+15>:	callq  0x1050 <puts@plt>
   0x0000000000001174 <+20>:	nop
   0x0000000000001175 <+21>:	pop    %rbp
   0x0000000000001176 <+22>:	retq   
End of assembler dump.
```
The fact that function addresses are managed in an array means that multiple functions can be registered and called in sequence.  

Therefore, by specifying <mark>priorities</mark> in individual attributes, a programmer can explicitly define the desired execution order.
### Priorities
```c
{
    "title": "Supporting priorities: ctor_pri.c",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>

// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor(101)))
void constructor_101() {
	printf("-> constructor priority 101\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor(102)))
void constructor_102() {
	printf("-> constructor priority 102\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor(101)))
void destructor_101() {
	printf("<- descriptor priority 101\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor(102)))
void destructor_102() {
	printf("<- descriptor priority 102\n");	
}

int main(void) {
	printf("main\n");
	return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Testing Priority values"
}
---
$ gcc -o ctor_pri ctor_pri.c
$ ./ctor_pri

// [!code highlight:2]
-> constructor priority 101
-> constructor priority 102
main
// [!code highlight:2]
<- descriptor priority 102
<- descriptor priority 101
```
For constructors, smaller priority values result in earlier execution.  

Conversely, for destructors, larger priority values result in earlier execution (at the beginning of the destruction phase) to take priority in the cleanup process.  

You can freely specify priority values of 101 or higher.
```adcard
```
What happens if multiple functions with the same priority are registered?
```c
{
    "title": "How are the same priorities handled?",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor(101)))
void ctor_func_1() {
	printf("-> constructor priority 101\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor(101)))
void ctor_func_2() {
	printf("-> me too constructor priority 101\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor(101)))
void dtors_func_1() {
	printf("<- descriptor priority 101\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor(101)))
void dtors_func_2() {
	printf("<- me too descriptor priority 101\n");	
}

int main(void) {
	printf("main\n");
	return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Testing Identical Priorities"
}
---
$ gcc -o ctor_pri_same ctor_pri_same.c
$ ./ctor_pri_same

// [!code highlight:2]
-> constructor priority 101
-> me too constructor priority 101
main
// [!code highlight:2]
<- me too descriptor priority 101
<- descriptor priority 101
```
From the output above, it is evident that when priority values are identical, functions are registered in the array in the order the source code was parsed and analyzed by the compiler, and they execute according to that sequential structure.  

The final <mark>destructor</mark> calls also strictly follow the LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) principle of a stack, ensuring that the destructor for the first-registered constructor is called last, in a safe reverse order.  

### Behavior on Abnormal Termination
What if an exceptional situation occurs where the program terminates abnormally via a <mark>Signal Handler</mark> or an <mark>exit</mark> system call, rather than a normal return from the `main` function?  

Let's further verify if the assigned destructor is correctly called even in these special cases.
```c
{
    "title": "Does it work in signal handler too?",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

// [!code highlight]
void sig_handler(int signo) {
	if (signo == SIGINT) {
		printf("received SIGINT\n");

		exit(0);
	}
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor))
void ctor_func() {
	printf("-> before main\n");	
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor))
void dtors_func() {
	printf("<- after main\n");	
}

int main(void) {
	signal(SIGINT, sig_handler);

	printf("main\n");

	while (1) {}

	return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Testing Signal Handler"
}
---
$ gcc sig.c -o sig
$ ./sig
-> before main
main
// [!code error]
^C received SIGINT
// [!code highlight]
<- after main
```
Even if a program is blocked in an infinite loop by `while (1)` and interrupted by an external `SIGINT` signal, explicitly calling <mark>exit(0)</mark> inside the signal handler ensures that `dtors_func()` is called normally with the intervention of the OS.
```adcard
```
### Leveraging in Shared Libraries
This constructor/destructor attribute control feature shows its true power when used with shared/dynamic libraries (<mark>Shared/Dynamic Library</mark>), not just in single executable files.  

Even for target applications already compiled and distributed as binaries, there's no need to rebuild them or modify their source code.  
By simply loading an external library equipped with constructors and destructors into memory (e.g., using <mark>LD_PRELOAD</mark>), you can easily inject desired code logic before and after the main function entry at any time.
```c
{
    "title": "Testing Shared Library (hooklib.c)",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor))
void ctor() {
	printf("hook lib constructor!\n");
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor))
void dtors() {
	printf("hook lib destructor!\n");
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Testing LD_PRELOAD"
}
---
// [!code word:shared]
// [!code word:LD_PRELOAD]
$ gcc -shared -o hooklib.so hooklib.c
$ LD_PRELOAD=`pwd`/hooklib.so ./pre-compiled
// [!code highlight]
hook lib constructor!
main
// [!code highlight]
hook lib destructor!
```
Of course, it can also be used directly in regular dynamic libraries linked to the application under development, not just with <mark>LD_PRELOAD</mark>.
```c
{
    "title": "Testing Shared Library Link (hooklib.c)",
    "ln": true
}
---
#include <stdio.h>

void foo(void) {
	printf("I'm foo!\n");
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((constructor))
void ctor() {
	printf("hook lib constructor!\n");
}
// [!code highlight]
__attribute__((destructor))
void dtors() {
	printf("hook lib destructor!\n");
}
```
```c
{
    "title": "sample.c"
}
---
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
	printf("I'm main\n");

	foo();
	return 0;
}
```
```bash
{
    "title": "Testing Shared Library Execution"
}
---
// [!code word:shared]
// [!code word:hooklib.so]
$ gcc -shared -o hooklib.so hooklib.c
$ gcc -o sample sample.c ./hooklib.so
$ ./sample
// [!code highlight]
hook lib constructor!
I'm main
// [!code highlight:2]
I'm foo!
hook lib destructor!
```
### Conclusion
As demonstrated, leveraging these built-in GCC compiler attributes allows for a variety of technical approaches.  

For example, it can be used as a safe backdoor hooking interface for indirect debugging of black-box binaries whose source code is lost or build is completed, or for optimization purposes such as preventing fatal memory leaks by controlling the dynamic allocation and deallocation of heavy global variables.
